Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 09/19/2024]
1.  Er = 851 - 2839 keV: measured resonance strengths are from Bosnjakovic et al. 1968 and Endt and van der Leun 1978. Note that no carefully measured (p,a) 
    strength exists for this reaction. No individual uncertainties are reported for the strengths. Bosnjakovic et al. 1968 state an overall uncertainty of 30%,
    which we adopt.
2.  Er 612 keV: we adopt the directly measured strength of Moazen et al. 2011.
3.  Er = 51, 167, 301 keV: Gp from (3He,d) experiment of Iliadis et al. 1994; partial width ratios from Ross et al. 1995; Gg = 0.5 eV is an inconsequential guess; 
    level lifetimes are not known.
4.  Er = 88, 234 keV: levels reported in Roepke et al. 2002, supposedly of negative parity and high spin, i.e., 4- or 5-. We assumed 5- for an upper limit estimation.
    Gg = 0.5 eV is a guess.
5.  Er 342 keV: level reported in Iliadis et al. 1996; 2+ (ell = 0) assumed for upper limit estimation. Gp is estimated assuming C2S < 0.0067 using (3He,d) 
    experiment of Iliadis et al. 1994. Ga upper limit is found assuming C2S_a < 0.01 (level not seen in (6Li,d) spectrum of Iliadis et al. 1994). Gg = 0.5 eV is a 
    guess. 
6.  Er = 382 keV: Jp = (<= 5-) reported; ell_p = 0 + 2, 1, and 0 can be excluded based on wg_pg comparison to directly measured upper limit. To estimate the upper
    limit contribution, we assumed natural parity, although unnatural parity is not excluded.
7.  Er = 404 keV: partial widths are estimated using measured wg_pg and wg_ag, the experimental limit on Gp from the experimental (3He,d) spectrum in Iliadis et al. 
    1996, the estimate C2S_a < 0.1 from the experimental (6Li,d) spectrum in Iliadis et al. 1996, and the estimate Gg < 1 eV.
8.  Er = 417 keV: Gp from (3He,d) information given in Iliadis et al. 1994. We assume that 8919 keV level (Roepke et al. 2002) is the same as the 8923 keV state 
    (Iliadis et al. 1994); spin/parity can then be restricted to Jp=(4,5)-. Stripping data are best described by l=3, although a small l=1 component cannot be 
    excluded; the l=1 component from fit yields a (p,g) resonance strength in excess of experimental upper limit; thus we assume pure l=3 transfer.
9.  Er = 434 keV: level has Jp = (2+, 3-). Transfer results and the directly measured (p,g) strength [Iliadis et al. 1994] are in much better agreement for 3-, which
    we adopted.    
10. Er = 510 keV: level has Jp = (3-, 4, 5-); (3He,d) angular distribution in Iliadis et al. 1994 is consistent with ell = 3 [Jp = 4-, 5-], although a small ell = 1
    component is not excluded [which would imply Jp = 3-]. Endt assumes Jp = 5-, based on analog assignments, which we will adopt. Gp is calculated from the 
    measured (p,g) strength in Iliadis et al. 1994 and Gg/G = 1 in Ross et al. 1995.
11. Er = 520 keV: level has Jp = 2; Endt assigns 2+ based on analog assignments, which we will adopt. Upper limit on Gp is estimated from (3He,d) spectrum in Iliadis
    et al. 1994. Gg = 0.5 eV is a guess.    
12. Er = 561 keV: Partial widths are estimated using measured (p,g) strength from Iliadis et al. 1994 and partial width ratios from Ross et al. 1995.    
13. Er = 612, 851 keV: Partial widths are calculated from measured (p,g), (p,a), (a,g) strengths.
14. Er = 627 - 837 keV: upper limit contributions are calculated using single-particle estimates for Gp and Ga.
15. Nonresonant S-factor describes low-energy tails of broad resonances in the Er = 1231-2194 keV region (information from Endt and van der Leun 1978). 
